# vmscan.env - the vmscan settings, as a split box keeps them in /etc/cti-agent/fleet.d/vmscan.env.
# Generated by scripts/lanes from fleet.env.example and settings.manifest: edit those.
# Lines starting with # are optional; set the others. Secrets are not here:
# store each with `sudo cti-agent-secrets set NAME` (cti-agent-onboard does it for you).

# The weekly Qualys VM scan report (run-vmscan): the two scheduled scans
# combined into one email, sent to the same people as the VM digest. Like the
# WAS lane it has its own pair and NO FALLBACK - unset means it refuses to send.
# The usual setting reuses the VM audience, by reference, so there is no second
# copy of the addresses to drift:
#
#     VMSCAN_TO=$VM_ALLOW_TO
#     VMSCAN_ALLOW_TO=$VM_ALLOW_TO
#
# (VMSCAN_TO is the list it is addressed to; VMSCAN_ALLOW_TO is the list those
# recipients are checked against. They are two settings so that neither can
# silently fall back to an audience nobody chose; a reference is the explicit
# way to say "the same people".)
#
# Which scans ran and which should have come from Qualys: the scan list and
# the scan schedules. Every scheduled scan that ran is reported and counts for
# the coverage check, and every active schedule that launched nothing in the
# week is reported missing, by its title in Qualys. VMSCAN_SCANS is rarely
# needed: extra titles to expect, comma separated, on top of the schedules.
# Leave it unset; stale titles are reported missing every week.
VMSCAN_TO=

VMSCAN_ALLOW_TO=

# The coverage check in that report: the network spreadsheet against the Asset
# Groups the scans cover. It needs to find the spreadsheet in SharePoint: the
# drive and item ids of the file (Graph shows them in the file's URL or API), and
# a Graph application permission that reaches it (Sites.Selected with a read
# grant on the site, or Files.Read.All). Leave them out and the check is skipped,
# and the report says so. The ids are specific to your tenant: set them here, in
# the root-owned file, never in the repository.
#SUBNETS_DRIVE_ID=

#SUBNETS_ITEM_ID=

#SUBNETS_SHEET=Sites

# Your answers about locations the sheet does not make clear (see cti-vmscan(1)).
#NETRULES_FILE=/etc/cti-agent/netrules.txt

# Findings from cross-checking the sheet against other records (an M&A
# network design, the incident-response contacts list), one "location | what
# to check" per line. Each goes to the network team's spreadsheet ticket, in
# its clean-up section, until you delete the line (see cti-vmscan(1)).
#NETNOTES_FILE=/etc/cti-agent/sheet-notes.txt

# Who the VM scan report asks why a network in the sheet is not scanned. Unset:
# the network team. Name everyone who adds networks, for example the team that
# brings in acquisitions: "network and M&A teams" (plural reads "are asked").
#VMSCAN_GAP_TEAMS="network team"

# How the network sheet marks a location's user network: its 10.X.<octet>
# network inside the location's /16, whatever the row is labelled. Unset: 30.
# off: only a row labelled corp counts. The report's sheet guide follows it.
#VMSCAN_USER_OCTET=30

# Row labels your sheet uses beyond the built-in ones (corp, security, netman,
# guest, voice, setup, inside, default, event, old-corp), comma-separated, so
# a label alone on its row is not read as a new location.
#VMSCAN_EXTRA_LABELS=
